  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 AM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: High pressure 1037 mb is currently centered over Nova \nScotia early this morning as a low level jet blows across Ontario \nand Quebec. This is resulting in clear skies and breezy conditions \non mountaintops. Temperatures this morning in the 30s and 40s will \nrebound into the lower 60s by the afternoon hours under southerly \nflow. A weak moisture starved cold front will slide south from \nCanada Monday night into Tuesday and wash out over the area with no \nprecipitation expected, though some morning clouds and cooler \ntemperatures can be expected Tuesday morning. Sunny skies will \nprevail thereafter with large scale ridging leading to low humidity \nand light winds through mid week. There continues to be high \nconfidence of no precipitation areawide at least through mid week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Little change in the forecast provided by WPC this \npackage with temps trending cooler than normal by late week and dry \nconditions prevailing. A weak cold front will drop south late Thurs \ninto Friday with northerly winds and cooler 925mb to 850mb thermal \nprofiles. The latest ECMWF is most aggressive with showing a closing \n700 to 500mb circulation and the potential for a few \nsprinkles/flurries, while the GFS/CMC are less robust with trof \ndevelopment and associated caa. The probability of measurable precip \nis <10% attm, so have no mention of pops in latest fcst. Based on \nprogged 850mb temps btwn 0C and -2C and similar 925mb temps, the \nhighs provided by WPC are in the upper 30s mtns summits to l/m 50s \nvalleys on Friday and warm into the mid 40s to upper 50s by Saturday \nand mostly mid 50s to lower 60s by Sunday. Lows generally in the mid \n20s to mid 30s, warming back into the mid 30s to lower 40s by \nSunday. As daily nocturnal inversion develop by next weekend, expect \na sharp thermal gradient between cooler/sheltered valleys and warmer \nmidslope/ridges to persist. These temperature details are not very \nwell captured by the latest forecast provided by WPC. \n\nFire weather concerns increase this week, given the dry conditions, \nmild temperatures and sunshine, significant drying of fine fuels, \nincluding leaf litter is expected, but winds are expected to remain \nlight.",
